# Entropy Power as Geometry

Start with the visual idea: after the right normalization, uncertainty has a radius, and two Gaussian sources form a right triangle.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Read more about [differential entropy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_entropy) and the [entropy power inequality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_power_inequality).

## The square-root transformation

Entropy power is $N_n(h)=e^{2h/n}/(2\pi e)$, while entropy radius is $r_n(h)=e^{h/n}/\sqrt{2\pi e}$. Hence $N_n(h)=r_n(h)^2$.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact Pythagorean equivalence</summary>
Substituting $N_n(h)=r_n(h)^2$ into $N_n(h_X)+N_n(h_Y)\le N_n(h_S)$ gives $r_n(h_X)^2+r_n(h_Y)^2\le r_n(h_S)^2$ with no loss in either direction.
</details>

{{visualization:0}}

## Locate the unique sharp boundary

The unique equality entropy is a scaled log-sum-exp. Explore how it changes with dimension and the two inputs.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal why the boundary is exact</summary>
At $B_n(h_X,h_Y)= rac n2\log(e^{2h_X/n}+e^{2h_Y/n})$, exponentiation cancels the logarithm and gives $N_n(B_n)=N_n(h_X)+N_n(h_Y)$. Strict monotonicity of entropy power makes this equality point unique and proves that the inequality holds exactly when $h_S\ge B_n$.
</details>

{{visualization:2}}

## Gaussian equality in every positive dimension

For a centered isotropic Gaussian of variance $v$, entropy is $ rac n2\log(2\pi ev)$, power is $v$, and radius is $\sqrt v$. Variances add under independent convolution.

{{demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

## Move above equality: exact stability

An entropy excess $\delta$ multiplies boundary power by $e^{2\delta/n}$. The deficit is therefore $(e^{2\delta/n}-1)(N_X+N_Y)$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the inverse stability conversion</summary>
If $P=N_X+N_Y$ and the deficit is $D$, then $\delta= rac n2\log(1+D/P)$. This translates a desired power margin directly into an entropy margin.
</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## Wider mathematical landscape

The radius language parallels the [Brunn--Minkowski inequality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brunn%E2%80%93Minkowski_theorem), where volume radii grow linearly under Minkowski addition. Here entropy radii combine quadratically, reflecting variance addition. Future work extends the scalar bridge to general measures, covariance matrices, and distributional stability.
